n^{7}\n\nA polynomial $P(q)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{m}{p}_{i}{q}^{i}$ is called parity-unimodal if the “even polynomial” ${\\sum }_{i=0}^{⌊m,/,2⌋}{p}_{2i}{q}^{i}$ and the “odd polynomial” ${\\sum }_{i=0}^{⌊(,m,-,1,),/,2⌋}{p}_{2i+1}{q}^{i}$ are both unimodal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParity-Unimodality and a Cyclic Sieving Phenomenon for Necklaces | SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/20M1370112)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: 2.4 years ago; By interpreting them as $\\mathfrak{sl}_2$-characters, we show that the rational $q$-Schröder polynomials are parity-unimodal, which means that the even- and odd-degree coefficients are separately unimodal. ... This is a special case of a more general cyclic sieving phenomenon for certain transitive $S_n$-actions, deduced from Molien's formula.\n\n# Parity-Unimodality and a Cyclic Sieving Phenomenon for Necklaces\n\nAuthor: Eric N. StuckyAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/20M1370112\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe discuss two surprising properties of a family of polynomials that generalize the Mahonian $q$-Catalan polynomials and, more generally, the $q$-Schröder polynomials. By interpreting them as $\\mathfrak{sl}_2$-characters, we show that the rational $q$-Schröder polynomials are parity-unimodal, which means that the even- and odd-degree coefficients are separately unimodal. Second, we show that they exhibit a $q=-1$ phenomenon. This is a special case of a more general cyclic sieving phenomenon for certain transitive $S_n$-actions, deduced from Molien's formula.\n\n### Keywords\n\n  1. combinatorics\n  2. Catalan\n  3. unimodal\n  4. cyclic sieving\n\n### MSC codes\n\n  1. 05E10\n  2. 16G99\n  3. 20C30\n\n## References\n\n1.\n\nD. Armstrong, N. A. Loehr, and G. S. Warrington, Rational parking functions and Catalan numbers, Ann. Comb., 20 (2016), pp. 21--58, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00026